== Children Showing Respect for Parents

=== §. 1. #emph[Of the generall Heads of Childrens duties.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[EPHES. 6.]

    #emph[1. #emph[Children obey your Parents in the Lord: for this is right.]]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

The second couple in a familie are

- Parents.
- Children.

In laying downe their duties, the Apostle beginneth with children: his direction, and instigation vnto them is laid downe in the three first verses of the sixt chapter: wherein

- 1\. He declareth their #emph[dutie.]
- 2\. He addeth #emph[reasons] to inforce the same.

In laying downe their dutie he noteth three points.

- 1\. #emph[Wherein] it consisteth, #emph[(obey, honour.)]
- 2\. #emph[To whom] it is to be performed #emph[(your parents.)]
- 3\. #emph[After what manner] it is to be done #emph[(in the Lord.)]

The reasons vsed by the Apostle are foure.

- 1\. The #emph[place] of parents #emph[(in the Lord.)]
- 2\. The #emph[aequitie] of the thing #emph[(this is right.)]
- 3\. The #emph[charge] of God #emph[(Honour thy father,] &c.)
- 4\. The #emph[reward] promised #emph[(That it may goe well,] &c.)

Vnder this word #emph[(obey)] which the Apostle vseth, and that word #emph[(honour)] which the law vseth, are all those duties comprised, which any where thorowout the whole Scripture are inioyned to children.

We will therefore set them downe in some order, and handle them distinctly one after another.

- 1\. The #emph[Fountaine] of childrens duties is to be searched out.
- 2\. The #emph[Streames] that flow from thence are to be obserued.

The #emph[Fountaine] is an inward disposition of the heart compounded of #emph[loue] and #emph[feare.]

The #emph[Streames] issuing from thence extend vnto parents, both while they are #emph[liuing,] and also when they are #emph[dead.]

Childrens duties which are to be performed to their parents while they liue, haue respect to their

+ #emph[Authoritie.]
+ #emph[Necessitie.]

The #emph[Authoritie] of parents requireth of children

+ #emph[Reuerence.]
+ #emph[Obedience.]

Their #emph[Necessitie] requireth #emph[Recompence.]

The duties which children owe to their parents deceased, respect their

+ #emph[Body.]
+ #emph[Credit.]

Their #emph[Body] with decency must be #emph[buried.]

Their #emph[Credit] with honour must be #emph[maintained.]

=== §. 2. #emph[Of Childrens loue to their Parents.]

I make the fountaine of childrens duties to be a mixed and compound disposition, in respect of that #emph[authoritie and affection] which is mixed together in parents. The #emph[authoritie] of parents requireth #emph[feare] in children: and their #emph[affection, loue.] So intire and so ardent is parents #emph[affection] towards their children, as it would make children too bold and insolent if there were not #emph[authoritie] mixed therewith to worke #emph[feare:] and so supreme and absolute is their #emph[authoritie] ouer them, as it would make children like slaues to dread their parents, if a fatherly #emph[affection] were not tempered therewith to breed #emph[loue.] But both these ioyned together make a very good composition: #emph[loue] like sugar sweetneth #emph[feare,] and #emph[feare] like salt seasoneth #emph[loue:] and thus, to ioyne them both together, it is a #emph[louing-feare,] or a #emph[fearingloue,] which is the ground of childrens duties.

Where Christ forbiddeth an excessiue loue in children to #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 10. 37.] their parents, he implieth that parents are a fit obiect for children to loue (so as their loue be wel moderated:) yea he implieth that it is an affection euen by nature ingraffed in children to loue their parents. #emph[Ioseph] is commended vnto children as a worthy patterne in louing his father, and that from his youth till the decease of his father: in testimony whereof in his younger yeeres he brought to his father the #emph[euill report of his] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 37. 2.] #emph[brethren,] whereby he incurred their enuie and hatred, which he would neuer haue done, if he had not loued his father: and hauing beene long absent from his father, when by Gods prouidence there was offered an occasion for him to meet with his brethren, one of his first questions to them was about their father: #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 43. 7.] and hearing that he was liuing, he thought it not enough to send him food for his need, but must also needs see his face, #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 45. 9.] and haue him dwell with him: and while his father was in the way he went out to meet him, and at first sight fell on his neck, #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 46. 29.] and wept a good while (a token of great affection.)

That loue which naturally parents beare to their children, #footnote[Reasons. 1. Equitie.] ought in equitie to breed in children a loue to their parents. For loue deserueth loue: and most vnworthy are they to be loued, who cannot loue againe. The loue of parents aboue all others is to be answered with loue on childrens part to the vttermost of their power, because it is free, great, and constant.

Besides, there is a necessitie of loue in children to their parents, #footnote[2 Necessitie.] lest for want thereof, their subiection (which of all others ought to be most free) should turne into slauish seruitude.

This ought children the rather to labour after, because by nature they are nothing so prone to loue their parents, as their parents are to loue them. Loue is weightie, and, as weighty things, it descendeth. Children therefore with conscience of dutie must labour to make supply of this defect, and helpe nature by grace. I denie not but naturally there is in children a greater loue to their parents, then to others: yet in comparison of the heat of parents loue to them, their loue to their parents is but cold. Wherfore as the heat of the Sun shining much and long on a stone wall, draweth a reflection of heat from that wall: so the hot beames of parents loue, which with feruency and constancy is cast on children, ought to prouoke and stirre vp children to send forth a reflection of loue on their parents.

Two extreames are contrary to this affection of loue.

One is #emph[want of naturall affection,] which is a vice most odious #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 1. 30. 2 #emph[Tim.] 3. 3.] and abominable in all, but most of all in children. The Apostle reckoneth this among the most heighnous vices that be.

The other is hatred and despight of parents: a vice more then monstrous, and vnnaturall. From thence commeth mocking and cursing of parents, whereof we shall afterwards heare.

=== §. 3. #emph[Of a childes feare of his parent.]

To the forenamed dutie of loue, must #emph[feare] be added, which is a childes #emph[awfull respect of his parent.]

This #emph[awfull respect] ariseth from an honourable esteeme which a childe in his iudgement and opinion hath of his parent, as he is his parent; and from it proceedeth on the one side, a desire and indeauour in all things to please the parent, and on the other side a loathnesse to offend him.

In this respect the feare of a #emph[childe] is opposed to the feare of a #emph[slaue.] For a childes feare being mixed with loue, hath respect to the #emph[offence] which a parent may take; but a slaues feare, which is ordinarily mixed with hatred, hath respect to nothing but the #emph[punishment] which his master may inflict vpon him. The forenamed feare is so proper to children, as that awfull respect which the Saints beare to God, is called a #footnote[See Treat. 1. §. 4.] #emph[siliall] or #emph[child-like feare.]

This #emph[feare] in a childe is an especiall branch of that #footnote[#emph[Exo.] 20. 12.] #emph[honour] which the law requireth of children to their parents: and it is in #footnote[#emph[Leu.] 19. 3.] expresse termes inioyned to children by the law. That phrase which God vseth of #emph[Miriam] (#footnote[#emph[Num.] 12. 14.] #emph[If her father had but spit in her face, should she not be ashamed seuen daies?)] sheweth that there ought to be such a feare of the parent in a childes heart, as should worke shame in it when the parent is offended.

A worthy patterne we haue hereof in #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 27. 12.] #emph[Iaakob,] who was loath to gaine the blessing with offence of his father.

This #emph[feare] keepeth #emph[loue] in compasse: and restraineth a childe #footnote[#emph[Reasons.] 1. Feare ordereth loue.] from ouermuch saucinesse, and malipartnesse.

And it is a cause of a childes reuerend and dutifull carriage #footnote[2\. It well guideth the carriage.] to his parent. For as the heart is affected the carriage will be ordered.

Contrary hereunto is that #emph[light,] or (which is more abominable) #footnote[Contrary, light and base esteeme of parents.] that #emph[base] and #emph[vile] esteeme of parents, which is in the heart of many children: especially if parents be poore, of low degree, vnlearned, ignorant, or subiect to any infirmities. It cannot be but that #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 9. 22.] #emph[Cham] had too light, if not a base esteeme of his father, when he derided him. A true filiall feare would haue restrained him from that extreme.

Wherefore to breed and cherish this feare, and to preuent, #footnote[How to breed and cherish a filiall feare.] or redresse the contrary extreme, let children well informe themselues of their parents place and authoritie, how they are in Gods stead, and a meanes vnder God of their childrens being: children haue receiued their very substance from the substance of their parents. In which respect though they should seeme contemptible to others, yet not to their children.

Thus much of a childes inward disposition towards his parent. The manifestation thereof must be by his outward carriage: and that in two things; #emph[Reuerence,] and #emph[Obedience:] both which respect a parents authoritie.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of a childes] Reuerence #emph[in refraining speech before his parent, and in hearkening to his parent.]

The outward #emph[reuerence] which children owe to their parents consisteth partly in their #emph[speech,] partly in their carriage.

Their speech both #emph[to] and #emph[of] their parents must sauour reuerence.

To their parents in presence.

OF their parents in absence.

In presence, by

+ #emph[Refraining]
+ #emph[Well framing]

their speech.

For refraining speech two vertues are requisite

+ #emph[Silence.]
+ #emph[Patience.]

Silence in

+ #emph[Forbearing] to speake.
+ #emph[Breaking] of speech. #footnote[Children ought to forbeare speaking in their parents presence].

Patience in hearkening to their parents.

The two branches of silence, in #emph[forbearing] to speake (especially when parents are speaking, or till parents giue leaue to their children to speake,) and in breaking off speech, when parents come into the place where children are speaking, are tokens of great reuerence. Thus children testifie that there are some in place whom they much respect and honour. #emph[Iob] doth thus set forth the respect which Princes and others did beare to him in his prosperitie, #emph[The Princes] (saith he) #emph[refrained] #footnote[#emph[Iob.] 29. 9, 10.] #emph[talking, and laid their hand vpon their mouth, the Nobles held their peace, &c.] Namely, while he was in presence, or while he spake. The like may be said of childrens patience in induring #footnote[Children must patiently hearken to their parents speech. #emph[Vers.] 25.] their parents speech; which #emph[Iob] also noteth in these words, #emph[Vnto me men gaue eare, and waited, and kept silence.] Though parents in their speech seeme to be long and tedious, yet must children indure it.

And it is very needfull that patience be added to silence, because many parents in tender loue of their children, and earnest desire of their good, thinke they can neuer speake enough in instructing and admonishing them. The many exhortations #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 49. 2. #emph[Pro.] 1. 8, #emph[&] 4. 1. #emph[&] 7. 1.] giuen in Scripture vnto children to #emph[heare, hearken, giue eare, giue heed, marke, and obserue] the words of their parents, doe imply the forenamed #emph[silence] and #emph[patience:] for they who #footnote[#emph[Iam] 1. 19.] ought to be #emph[swift to heare] must be #emph[slow to speake.] I denie not but much more is intended vnder those phrases, namely, #emph[obedience:] yet must #emph[these] also be presupposed: for he that will not in silence patiently hearken to his parents while they speake, will much lesse obey what they say.

Contrary to silence is sawcinesse (as we speake) and ouermuch #footnote[Contrary, 1. boldnesse of speech.] boldnesse in children, when, without due respect of their parents presence, they will be prating of this thing or that thing: insomuch as if strangers should come into the roome where such children are, they would not thinke that their parents were in presence; or if they knew it, they might well thinke that such children beare little respect to their parents.

Contrary to patience in hearing, is fretting and murmuring #footnote[2\. Impatient hearkening to parents.] against parents (if at least their speech be any whit long) and flinging or slinking away before they haue done. These faults are the greater, if children by their loquacitie, or impatiencie hinder or interrupt their parents speech when they are giuing any admonition or instruction: for thus they shew both too light a respect of their parents: and also too little regard of the meanes of their owne good.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of a childes reuerend framing his speech to his parent.]

A childes reuerence in well framing his speech to his parents #footnote[A childs reuerence in speech is manifested, 1. By reuerend titles.] may many waies be manifested, as

1. By giuing vnto them reuerend and honourable titles. No title can be more honourable then that which is most proper and vsuall, #emph[Father] to the one parent, and #emph[Mother] to the other. God taketh the title #footnote[#emph[Jer.] 31. 9. #emph[Gal.] 4. 6.] #emph[Father] vnto himselfe, as a title of great dignity.

#strong[#emph[Obiect.] This title is so proper to God, as #footnote[#emph[Matt.] 23. 9.] #emph[We are to call none in earth Father.]]

#emph[Answ.] This is not simply to be taken of the title it selfe, but of the minde of him that giueth or affecteth that title.

If it be affected or giuen to obscure Gods #emph[Fatherhood,] or to make a man #emph[a Father] of himselfe without dependance on God, or reference to him, who is properly the father of all, it is an impious and sacrilegious title. But otherwise lawfull and warrantable.

In Scripture the title #emph[Father] is giuen to all degrees of dignities among men, as to #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 24. 12.] #emph[Kings,] #footnote[2 #emph[Kin.] 5. 13.] #emph[Captaines,] and other #emph[chiefe Gouernours,] to #footnote[#emph[Judg.] 18. 19.] #emph[Priests,] #footnote[2 #emph[King.] 6. 21.] #emph[Prophets,] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 4. 15.] #emph[Apostles,] and other #footnote[1 #emph[Tim.] 5. 1.] #emph[Ministers.] In the fift commandement all superiours are comprised vnder it, therefore #emph[Father] is a title of great honour: and by the rule of relation #emph[Mother] is a title of as great honour to the female sex. Religious and dutifull children haue euer vsed to giue those titles to their parents. #emph[My Father,] saith #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 22. 7.] #emph[Isaak] to #emph[Abraham,] and #footnote[#emph[&] 27. 18.] #emph[Iaakob] to #emph[Isaak.] #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 2. 20.] #emph[My Mother,] saith #emph[Salomon] to #emph[bathsheba.] I finde also the title of #footnote[#emph[Matt.] 21. 30.] #emph[Sir] or #emph[Lord,] vsed: a title #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 31. 35.] of honour.

2. By vsing #emph[few words] before their parents: and those few #footnote[2\. By few words.] not without iust occasion, being first spoken to by their parents, or hauing leaue of them, or making knowne to them me needfull matter: at least not against their parents liking, as their parents should be offended thereby. And if they obserue their parents to be vnwilling to heare them speake any more of such and such a matter, then ought they to lay their hands vpon their mouthes, as #emph[Isaak (Gen.] 22. 7.) and #emph[Iaakob (Gen.] 27. 12.) This is a token of great respect.

3. By meeke and humble speeches. Such was the speech of #footnote[3\. By humble speech.] #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 19 4.] #emph[Ionathan] the naturall sonne of #emph[Saul,] and of #footnote[#emph[&] 24. 10.] #emph[Dauid] his son in law, wherewith he was much contented, and his wrath pacified.

4. By obseruing a fit opportunity: as when parents are not #footnote[4 By seasonable speech.] seriously busied, or in company, or in passion. When #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 19. 6.] #emph[Saul] was out of passion, how well did he accept #emph[Ionathans] Apologie for #emph[Dauid?] but #footnote[#emph[&] 20. 30.] in his passion, how ill did he take it? This wise obseruing of fit opportunity sheweth great reuerence.

5. By a present, ready, willing, pleasing answer, when by #footnote[5\. By ready answers.] their parents they shall be spoken vnto. #emph[Eli] was as a father to #emph[Samuel:] therefore when #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 3. 4, 6,] #emph[Samuel] thought that #emph[Eli] called him, he presently and readily answered, #emph[Here am I:] and when #emph[Eli] was instant to know what the Lord had said to him, #footnote[18.] #emph[Samuel told him euery whit, and held nothing from him.] #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 21. 30.] The younger son (noted in the parable) shewed a sonne-like reuerence in giuing a willing and ready answer to his father, though he failed in his obedience, by not performing what he promised.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of the vices in children contrary to the forenamed reuerence in speech.]

Contrary to those branches of reuerence in speech are, #footnote[Contrary, 1. Scorning to acknowledge a parent.]

1. #emph[Pride:] when children scorne to giue the title of #emph[Father,] or #emph[Mother,] to their parents. This is the minde of many who haue gotten more wealth or honour, then euer their parents had. In publicke especially such children most refuse to giue those titles. #emph[Salomon] was not so minded. He being a great king, sitting vpon his throne, in sight and hearing of all his people that were about him, called #emph[Bathsheba Mother.] #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 2. 20.]

If children had that regard to the honour of their parents which they should, they being themselues in places of honour and dignity, would the rather openly call their parents #emph[Father] and #emph[Mother,] that they might be knowne to be the father and mother of so eminent a person.

2. #emph[Loquacity,] and too much importunity, or rather impudency #footnote[2\. Too much importunitie.] in speech, when children hauing to doe with their parents, can neuer haue done (as we speake) but must needs vse matters to the very vttermost. Many parents are oft much prouoked hereby. It skilleth not that the childe haue the right, especially in a matter of no great consequence. For reuerence sake the childe must forbeare, at least for a time. And if the matter of difference be weighty, as in points of religion, the child must either take some other opportunity of better informing his parent, or else get some other wise friend to doe it.

3. #emph[Stoutnesse,] when children answer their parents as if they #footnote[3\. Stout answers. #emph[Ne filius cum patre quoquo modo expostulet, cum lex interdixit, tum anteleges ipsa natura. Greg. Naz. in senten. Matt.] 21. 29. #emph[Luke] 15. 29.] were their equals: giuing word for word. It doth as ill become children to #emph[answer againe,] as seruants (to whom the Apostle hath expresly forbidden it, #emph[Tit.] 2. 9.) Both law and nature forbiddeth children to be prouoked hereunto, by any thing that their parents say or doe; how great then is their fault who giue scornefull and stout words to their parents when they are no way prouoked, as the #emph[elder sonne] noted in the parable, and the #emph[elder brother] of the prodigall childe?

4. #emph[Indiscretion,] when children haue no respect to any time, businesse, or temper, of their parents in speaking to them, and #footnote[4\. Vnseasonable speech. #emph[Ephes.] 6. 4.] so, much prouoke them. It is laid downe as a caueat to parents, that they #emph[prouoke not their children to wrath.] How much more must children obserue that cauear?

5. #emph[Stubbornesse,] when children pout, loure, swell, and giue #footnote[5\. Stomachfull silence.] to answer at all to their parents. This is too common a fault in children, and many parents are much offended and grieued thereat. We heard #footnote[§. 4.] before of a childelike silence which was very commendable, and a token of great reuerence; but this is worthy of much blame, a token of great vndutifulnesse; and carefully to be avoided, as that to be practised.

=== §. 7. #emph[Of childrens reuerend speeches of their parents.]

So true and intire ought that reuerend respect to be which children beare to their parents, as their speech not only #emph[to them] before their faces, but also #emph[of them] behinde their backes, must be so framed both for matter and manner, when they haue any occasion to fall into speech of their parents, as all that heare them may note them to beare a reuererend respect to their parents. #footnote[Direction for children how to speake of their parents.]

As a generall direction for the better performing of this duty, let children speake nothing of their parents that they would be loath should come to their parents eare. More particularly, let them speake of those things which most tend to their commendation, that so (as Christ said of his father #footnote[#emph[Iohn] 8. 49.]) they may honour their parents. Let other things be buried in silence so much as in them lieth. And if others speake of matters disgracefull to their parents, let them interpret in the better sense things doubtfull, and, so farre as they may, extenuate things euident, and sharply reproue them that slander their parents. This is that #emph[blessing] which children owe to their parents, for neglecting whereof the wiseman taxeth children saying, #emph[There is a generation that doth not blesse their] #footnote[#emph[Prou.] 30. 11.] #emph[mother.]

Contrary to that kinde of blessing is discouering of parents #footnote[Childrens vnreuerend speeches of their parents.] infirmities, noted in cursed #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 9. 22.] #emph[Cham,] and broaching vntruths of them, noted in impious #footnote[2 #emph[Sam.] 15. 3.] #emph[Absolom,] and mocking and cursing them #footnote[#emph[Prou.] 30. 11.] expresly condemned. The reward whereof is by #footnote[#emph[Leu.] 20. 9.] Gods law death: yea a shamefull and ignominious death, for the #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 30. 17.] #emph[Rauens of the valley shall plucke out his eies, and the young Eagles shall eat it:] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 40. 19.] which phrase setteth forth the end of a notorious malefactor that is hanged.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of a childs reuerend cariage to his parent.]

As the speech, so the cariage of children towards their parents must be seasoned with reuerence: for

- 1\. #emph[This] is a fruit, and proofe of filiall feare as well as #emph[that.]
- 2\. Of the two, this is the surer euidence: for actions are better signes of the disposition of the heart then words.
- 3\. Faire words ioyned with contrary deeds, cannot but be accounted meerely complementall and hypocriticall.
- 4\. Where there is a contrariety betwixt words and deeds, the one will be a witnesse against the other, and that mans condemnation the greater. #footnote[Childrens reuerence to their parents manifested, 1. by meeting them.]

Wherefore let all reuerence be manifested in childrens behauiour to their parents, and that in these and such like instances.

1. If a parent be comming to a childe, and the childe obserue it, let him haste to meet his parent: so did #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 46. 29.] #emph[Ioseph] to his father, and #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 2. 19.] #emph[Salomon] to his mother. Which two examples are the rather to be noted, because both were in eminent place: one a great gouernour, the other a king.

2. Let such child-like obeisance be performed as becommeth #footnote[2\. By obeysance to them.] the age and sex, either in going to, remaining before, or going from a parent: as vncouering the head, bending the knee, bowing the body, standing vp, with the like. The two forenamed eminent persons, #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 48. 12.] #emph[Ioseph] and #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 2. 19.] #emph[Solomon,] bowed, the one to his father, the other to his mother.

3. Let the countenance, and gesture of the body be so soberly #footnote[3 By well composing the countenance.] and modestly ordered in the presence of the parent, as may argue due respect.

4. Let the vpper place, and hand be giuen to parents: and #footnote[4\. By giuing the vpper hand.] if occasionally a childe be aboue his parent, let him come below him. For that is a manifest token of inferiority and subiection. What maketh men to striue for the vpper hand, but because they would be accounted better then those with whom they striue? But that ought not to be the minde of children to their parents.

#strong[#emph[Quest.] What if children be in estate more wealthy, or honourable then their parents, are they then to giue the hand to them?]

#emph[Answ.] No honour is comparable to the dignity of fatherhood: it giueth a greater eminency to the parent ouer his childe, then any other honour can to the childe ouer his parent. I grant that a childe may by some office, and outward dignity be so aduanced aboue his father, as other men may more honour and reuerence the childe, and giue the vpper place to him: and for order sake the childe may and ought to take it in company: but when they are alone, the childe must rather reuerence the father.

5. According to the custome of the time and place wherein #footnote[5\. By asking blessing.] they liue, let children aske their parents blessing.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of childrens asking their parents blessing, whether it be lawfull or no.]

Some doubt is made of this duty both in regard of the thing it selfe, and also of the gesture of kneeling vsed in the performance thereof: I will therefore distinctly proue both.

For the thing, it is noted of #emph[Iaakob] that he carried sauory meat to his father, that #emph[he might blesse him:] and of #emph[Ioseph,] that #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 27. 19.] he went to his father, and carried his two sonnes with him, #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 48. 1. #emph[&c.]] that his father might #emph[blesse] both him and them: for which end the twelue sonnes of #emph[Iaakob] assembled to their father. #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 49. 1. #emph[&c.] How the blessings which the patriarchs gaue their children are patterns to others.]

#emph[Obiect.] These were extraordinary examples: the Patriarchs were indued with the spirit of prophesie, whereby they reuealed to their children what their estate should be in the times to come: for knowledge whereof their children came to them.

1. #emph[Answ.] Their blessings were more then predictions of things to come: they were confirmations and assurances to the #footnote[See Treat. 6. §. 58.] children that God would indeed performe that blessing which their parents had pronounced. For they sustained a double person: the person of a Prophet, and of a father; as prophets they foretold things to come: as fathers they obtained the blessings pronounced, and an assurance thereof to their children, #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 11. 20.] and that by faith and praier.

2. #emph[Answ.] Though all parents cannot with such an extraordinary spirit assure vnto their children any distinct particular blessing, yet the faithfull praier of parents is an especiall, and ordinary meanes to obtaine a blessing from God vpon their children: and that because of Gods promise which extends #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 17. 7. #emph[Act.] 2. 39.] it selfe not only to fathfull parents, but also to their seed. Wherefore as the children of the patriarches came to their fathers to be assured of some extraordinary blessing, so may other children goe to their parents as a meanes to obtaine an ordinary blessing. It is noted of #emph[Eliah] that by an extraordinary spirit in praier he obtained extraordinary matters: Yet #footnote[#emph[Iam.] 5. 17.] the Apostle setteth forth that example to all Christians as a motiue to stirre them vp in faith to pray for ordinary blessings. But for further clearing of this point, note the phrase vsed in the fift commandement as a reason to moue children to honour their parents: this it is word for word, #emph[That they may prolong thy daies, &c.] how can parents prolong their childrens daies, but by begging that blessing of God? #footnote[See Treat. 6. §. 4. #emph[Prou.] 15. 8. How wicked parents may blesse their children.] The praiers then of parents are a great blessing to children, and children ought to seeke this blessing of their parents.

#emph[Obiect.] If parents be wicked, their praier is abomination: what blessing then can children looke for from wicked parents?

#emph[Answ.] Though God heare not wicked parents in loue and goodnesse to themselues, yet for the good of their children he may and will heare them: and that the rather to maintaine a reuerend respect of parents in the heart of their children. For asking a blessing is an acknowledgement of superiority and authority, according to that of the Apostle, #emph[The lesse is blessed] #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 7. 7.] #emph[of the greater.]

Concerning the gesture of kneeling, it is answerable to the #footnote[It is lawfull for children to kneele to their parents. #emph[Gen.] 48. 12.] gesture which of old was vsed by Gods people in like case: of #emph[Ioseph] it is said that #emph[he bowed downe himselfe with his face to the earth.]

#strong[#emph[Obiect.] Kneeling is a gesture proper to Gods worship.]

#emph[Answ.] It is not so proper, but that it may be vsed in ciuill cases: else Christ would haue reproued the young man for #emph[kneeling] before him as well as for calling him #emph[good:] for he conceiued #footnote[#emph[Mar.] 10. 17.] Christ to be but a meere man, and the worship he did him was but ciuill.

It is not simply the gesture, but the occasion of the gesture, the minde of him that performeth it, and the end why he performeth it that maketh it diuine, or ciuill. #emph[Cornelius] fell down before #emph[Peter] with conceit of some diuine excellency in him, and was not allowed: his manner of worshiping was diuine. The iaylor fell downe before #emph[Paul] and #emph[Silas] in acknowledgement of some outward eminency in them, and was not reproued: his manner of worshipping was meerely ciuill. The same gesture may be performed to different persons with a different respect. A childe may kneele to his parent, and to the king. Yet it followeth not that he maketh his parent a king. Neither will it follow that by kneeling to his parent he maketh him a God, because men kneele to God.

=== §. 10. #emph[Of the vices contrary to childrens reuerend gesture towards their parents.]

Contrary to the forenamed branches of reuerend gesture, are, #footnote[Vnreuerend gestures of children.]

1. #emph[Rudenesse] and #emph[vnmannerlinesse,] when children know not how to put difference betwixt their parents and strangers, but can #footnote[1\. Rudenesse.] suffer their parents to come to them, and they abide in their place and not stirre to meet them.

2. #emph[Disdainfull statelinesse,] when they thinke much to stand #footnote[2\. Statelinesse.] bare-headed any while in their parents presence. It falleth out many times, that when parents and children are together before their betters, #emph[they] will shew more reuerence then #emph[these:] for the father will stand, and be vncouered, when the sonne sitteth downe and puts on his hat, vpon conceit that his father doth more reuerence then is meet: but if it were so, yet the sonne for the fathers sake should stoope somewhat the lower.

3. #emph[Wantonnesse] and #emph[boldnesse,] when children are ouer-familiar #footnote[3\. Boldnesse.] with their parents: toying and gigling vpon euery light occasion. This kinde of cariage cannot but much tend to the disgrace and dishonour of parents. For what can they who behold it thinke, but that such children haue beene too much cockered and ill nurtered?

4. #emph[Ambition,] when children are so ambitiously desirous of place, #footnote[4\. Ambition.] especially in companie, as rather then be vnder some whom they suppose to be at least their equals, they will be aboue their parents. This oft falleth out, when parents being of a lowly minde, giue place to such as their children, being of a lofty minde, thinke meaner then themselues. Now rather then they will be vnder their inferiours (as they suppose) they will be aboue their parents. A point of great insolencie. Such ought to be the respect of a childe to his parent, as he should debase himselfe below those that are his inferiours, rather then exalt himselfe aboue his parent. As with other men, for peace sake, in many cases, a man must depart from his right; so especially with his parent, in case of superioritie. Would not euery one that knowes what honour a childe owes to a father, condemne that childs ambition, that should so stand vpon the place and hand, as to take them of his parent?

5. An #emph[ouer-nice] and erroneous #emph[opinion] of those, who thinke it #footnote[5\. Refusing to aske blessing.] vnmeet for any childe to aske their parents blessing. Their owne conceit more swayeth them, then the continuali approued practise of Gods people in all ages: not vnlike him whom #emph[Solomon] saith to be #emph[wiser in his owne conceit, then seuen men that can render] #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 26. 16.] #emph[a reason.] Others, though they doe not so generally disallow this dutie, yet they thinke it meet only for yong children: not considering of what yeeres, stature, and state, #emph[Ioseph] was, when he performed it.

As for those, who thinke it not vnlawfull, yet carelesly neglect it, they little consider the benefit of a parents blessing. Profane #emph[Esau] shall another day rise vp in iudgement against them. He #footnote[#emph[Gen] 27. 34. #emph[Heb.] 12 17.] beggd and beggd againe and againe, and that with a loud cry and salt teares, a blessing of his father.

Thus much of childrens reuerence. Their obedience followeth.
